  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA large winter storm system continues to bring precip to the area \ntoday. A large upper level low continues to track along the TN, AL, \nGA border this afternoon before tracking northeast. The sfc low \ncontinues to track north along the eastern seaboard. Moisture \ncontinues to wrap around the low and stream in from the Atlantic. \nThis wrap around moisture is bringing us our continued precip today, \nmainly in the form of rain, with only a few spots still seeing snow. \nPrecip will taper off gradually from west to east tonight and\ninto early tomorrow morning. That being said, there is a slight\nconcern regarding the potential for black ice overnight for \nportions of SEMO as temperatures get down to right around \nfreezing. Additional rainfall totals will range from 0.25-0.50\" \nsouthwest to 0.5-1.00\" northeast. Winds will continue to be \nbreezy out of the northwest today with a tight sfc gradient; \nwinds should relax more tomorrow through the day. \n\nBehind this system brief ridging brings warming temperatures Friday \nand Saturday. Highs climb back into the 50s by Saturday. Late \nSaturday a longwave trof begins to take shape aloft over the Plains \nproducing a lee low a the sfc. As this trof aloft shifts east, the \nlow tracks through OK, northern AR, and is over the Quad State by \nSunday morning. This system looks to bring our next chance (70-100%) \nfor precip starting Saturday night. \n\nThis system will be moving through rather quickly, so moisture \nreturns will be limited but midlevel lapse rates will increase to \naround 6.5-7.5 degC/km which would be sufficient for thunderstorms, \nbut instability looks minimal with dewpoints only in the upper mid \n40s to lower 50s. Behind this quick hitting system, midlevel ridging \nand sfc high pressure fill in and bring back dry weather for next \nweek with temperatures warming to above average by midweek.",
